--TEST--
Core\IO::lines' pieces rebuild the file Core\IO::readText answers whole, over a final terminator, a file without one, a lone terminator and an empty file — and the one place the two members part is the decode, where a second charset changes every row and no line boundary — spec § 14, ADR 0009 § 1
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The two members are `slurp` reached twice: the same door, the same buffer,
// and then a split in one and a decode in the other. The existing agreement
// case checks `lines` against `Core\Str::lines`, which is the *same splitter*
// -- so a splitter wrong about a trailing terminator is wrong on both sides of
// it and still agrees. `readText` is the independent oracle: it answers the
// file undivided, so re-joining the pieces has to reproduce it exactly, and an
// invented or dropped final empty line cannot cancel out.
//
// The four bodies are the four ways a file can end, which is where a splitter
// is off by one if it is off at all.
array<string> $bodies = [
    "one\ntwo\nthree\n",
    "no terminator at all",
    "\n",
    "",
];
int $total = Core\Arr::count($bodies) as int;
int $rebuilt = 0;
int $pieces = 0;

int $i = 0;
for (; $i < $total; $i = $i + 1) {
    Core\IO::write("subject.txt", $bodies[$i]);
    string $text = Core\IO::readText("subject.txt");

    array<string> $walked = [];
    foreach (Core\IO::lines("subject.txt") as string $line) {
        $walked[] = $line;
        $pieces = $pieces + 1;
    }

    // The reconstruction rule, which is the whole claim: the pieces joined by
    // the terminator, plus the terminator itself exactly when the file ended
    // with one. A trailing terminator closes the last line rather than opening
    // an empty one, so it is spelled here and not counted as a piece.
    string $joined = Core\Str::join($walked, "\n");
    if (Core\Str::endsWith($text, "\n")) {
        $joined = $joined . "\n";
    }
    if ($joined == $text) {
        $rebuilt = $rebuilt + 1;
    }
}

echo "bodies: ", $total as string,
     ", rebuilt from their own lines: ", $rebuilt as string,
     ", pieces: ", $pieces as string, "\n";

// And the divergence, which is one thing and not several: `lines` is `read`'s
// treatment of the octets, so it agrees with the reading `readText` makes
// under the charset the file was written in and with no other. The three
// terminators are ASCII, so the second charset moves every row's *content*
// and no row's *boundary* -- a member that decoded on its own would agree
// where this counts a disagreement, and one that split on a high byte would
// move the count.
Core\IO::write("subject.txt", "café\nnaïve\n");
array<string> $asWritten = Core\Str::lines(Core\IO::readText("subject.txt"));
array<string> $asLatin1 = Core\Str::lines(Core\IO::readText("subject.txt", {charset: Core\Charset::Latin1}));

int $rows = Core\Arr::count($asWritten) as int;
int $sameRows = 0;
int $changed = 0;
int $at = 0;

foreach (Core\IO::lines("subject.txt") as string $line) {
    if ($at < $rows) {
        if ($line == $asWritten[$at]) {
            $sameRows = $sameRows + 1;
        }
        if ($line != $asLatin1[$at]) {
            $changed = $changed + 1;
        }
    }
    $at = $at + 1;
}

echo "accented rows: ", $rows as string,
     ", walked: ", $at as string,
     ", agreeing with the reading it was written under: ", $sameRows as string,
     ", changed by the second charset: ", $changed as string, "\n";

// Both charsets found the same number of rows, which is the boundary half of
// the same claim said as one line rather than left to be read off two.
int $moved = (Core\Arr::count($asLatin1) as int) - $rows;
echo "boundaries the charset moved: ", $moved as string, "\n";

Core\IO::remove("subject.txt");
--FILE nvs.toml--
[capabilities.fs]
read = ["."]
write = ["."]
--EXPECT--
bodies: 4, rebuilt from their own lines: 4, pieces: 5
accented rows: 2, walked: 2, agreeing with the reading it was written under: 2, changed by the second charset: 2
boundaries the charset moved: 0
